{
  "text": "Watch this scroll. One frame just arrived 8 milliseconds late. You can't see it. But your thumb can feel it. On an iPhone, that almost never happens. That is not luck. To make a scroll this smooth, Apple designed its own chips, its own modem and its own software, and spent 125 million dollars a day on research last year. So let's open it up, layer by layer, all the way down to a single transistor. Start with the screen. It can redraw 120 times a second. So every frame has a deadline: about 8.3 milliseconds. Miss it, and the old picture stays on screen for one more beat. Engineers call that a hitch. Here is the race. Your finger moves, and the touch goes to the app. The app works out what should change, then hands it over. A separate system process, the render server, prepares the frame. The graphics chip paints it. And the display shows it on the very next tick. And here is the clever part. Animations run inside that render server, outside the app. So even when an app is busy, the motion on screen can keep going. And when nothing moves, ProMotion slows the screen right down to save battery. But software alone cannot win that race 7,200 times a minute. It needs a chip built for exactly this software. And in 2008, Apple did not design a single one. So it bought a small chip company, P.A. Semi, for 278 million dollars. Two years later came the A4, the first chip Apple designed itself. In 2013, the A7 became the first 64-bit chip in a phone. In 2017, the A11 added a Neural Engine for machine learning. And in 2020, the same idea, scaled up, became the M1, and replaced Intel inside the Mac. Then Apple went after the chips around it. Its own modem, the C1, in 2025. Its own wireless chip, the N1, the same year. And now, in the iPhone 18 Pro, the A20 Pro: Apple's first 2 nanometer chip. Eighteen years of work, for one feeling under your thumb. So where does all of it actually sit? Let's open the phone.",
